---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import BenchmarkSummary from "../components/BenchmarkSummary.astro";
import ScenarioChart from "../components/ScenarioChart.astro";
import EvidenceLinks from "../components/EvidenceLinks.astro";
import { BLOB, href } from "../site";
---
<Base
  path="/benchmarks/"
  title="JavaScript Signals Benchmark: Reflex Quant vs alien-signals vs Reflex"
  headline="JavaScript Signals Benchmark"
  description="Reactivity benchmark results for Reflex Quant against alien-signals and Reflex on 18 scenarios, with methodology, structure counters and CI history."
>
  <p class="lede">Eighteen scenarios, three runtimes, one runner per comparison. Where Quant wins, where it loses, and why each of those is the expected result of how it is built.</p>

  <h2>Results</h2>
  <BenchmarkSummary />
  <ScenarioChart />

  <h2>Where Quant is ahead</h2>
  <ul class="fingers">
    <li><strong>Batched writes</strong> (<code>batch.writes-32</code>, 0.55 of the better rival). A batch defers watcher delivery only; there is no transaction to open or commit.</li>
    <li><strong>Shared dynamic graphs</strong> (<code>shared-chaotic.64x32</code>, 0.78; 0.22 of alien-signals). Reads arrive in a different order every pass, and Quant moves relations where alien-signals relinks them.</li>
    <li><strong>Branch switching</strong> (<code>branch.switch-100</code>, 0.87) and <strong>replacing half the sources</strong> (<code>replace-half.256</code>, 0.91). A relation is allocated only when it is new.</li>
    <li><strong>Effect fan-out</strong> (<code>fan-out.effects-1000</code>, 0.88) and <strong>diamonds</strong> (<code>diamond.8x8</code>, 0.93).</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where it is behind</h2>
  <ul class="fingers">
    <li><strong>Sorting by value</strong> (<code>value-sort.256x32</code>, 1.43 of Reflex) and <strong>every reorder scenario</strong> (1.20 to 1.21 of Reflex). Reflex's tracking is specialized for permutations. Quant's is general: bounded scans, then one linear walk per pass. That is cheaper to specify and check, and slower on a pure permutation.</li>
    <li><strong>The trivial paths</strong>: <code>static.read-clean</code> (1.22 of alien-signals) and the stable wide reads (1.17 of Reflex). A read of a clean value is almost all call overhead, and there is the least to save there.</li>
    <li><strong>Computed fan-out</strong> (<code>fan-out.computeds-1000</code>, 1.18 of Reflex).</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Against alien-signals alone the reorder scenarios are the widest gap in Quant's favour, at 0.15 to 0.17 of its time. That one is structural, and the counters on the <a href={href("/dynamic-dependency-tracking/")}>dependency tracking</a> page show it without a clock.</p>

  <h2>Method</h2>
  <ul class="fingers">
    <li><strong>CI only.</strong> Timing runs on GitHub Actions for every build. Local timing is not reported.</li>
    <li><strong>Ratios within one runner.</strong> The scenarios run in shards, one job each, and every job times all three runtimes on the same machine. A hosted runner is not a fixed CPU, so times are never compared across jobs; only ratios inside one.</li>
    <li><strong>Fresh process per pair.</strong> Every (runtime, scenario) pair runs in its own process each round, in random order, for 9 rounds. A round's value is the median of 21 batches of about 10 ms, after at least 300 ms and 2,000 steps of warm-up.</li>
    <li><strong>Same answers first.</strong> Before any timing, the first 64 steps of every scenario must produce the same checksum on all three runtimes. A runtime cannot win by computing something else.</li>
    <li><strong>Fixed rivals.</strong> Reflex is a frozen production bundle of commit <code>bf71877</code>, behind the same minimal host Quant uses; alien-signals is 3.2.1.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What these numbers are not</h2>
  <p>The suite is this project's own, chosen to cover its task list. It is not a general ranking of signals libraries, and the numbers move between runs: an earlier run had Quant within 25 % of the better rival on all 18 scenarios with a geometric mean of 0.97; the one shown here has 17 of 18 and 1.03. The trajectory over every CI run is in <a href={`${BLOB}/research/HISTORY.md`}>HISTORY.md</a>.</p>
  <p>Benchmarks also reward skipped work. A runtime can win a scenario by not doing something its users rely on. Quant's timings count only for a build that passes its differential tests, so speed is measured against fixed semantics. In this project a benchmark may choose between two correct implementations. It may not change what correct means.</p>
  <EvidenceLinks items={["bench", "report", "history"]} />
</Base>
